CONC = [
("h1", "Conclusion"),
("p", "The volume began with a plain statement: the prophets give the tests themselves, and the tests are not one rule restated. It has tried to show that from the prophets’ own sentences. A prophet is the LORD’s appointed spokesman, and the words are put in his mouth (Numbers 12:6–8; Deuteronomy 18:18). A word is known first by where it came from: out of the mouth of the LORD, or out of the prophet’s own heart (Jeremiah 23:16; Ezekiel 13:2). It is refused if it leads Israel from the LORD, even when its sign comes true (Deuteronomy 13:1–5). An announced event that does not come to pass was not the LORD’s word (Deuteronomy 18:22), unless the LORD attached a condition and the people met it (Jeremiah 18:7–10; Jonah 3:10). And a word the LORD has sworn, or has marked as one he will not turn back, stands (Isaiah 54:9, 10; Malachi 3:6; Ezekiel 24:14)."),
("ctbl", "The position in one table.", [
    ("Question", "The position", "Chief passages"),
    ("Who is a prophet?", "One the LORD makes himself known to and sends; the words are his", "Num 12:6–8; Deut 18:18"),
    ("Where did the word come from?", "Out of the mouth of the LORD, not the prophet’s own heart", "Jer 23:16, 21; Ezek 13:2, 6"),
    ("Where does it lead?", "A word that leads from the LORD is refused, sign or no sign", "Deut 13:1–5; Jer 28:15, 16"),
    ("Did it come to pass?", "If not, the LORD did not speak it; the test acts after the event", "Deut 18:22; 1 Kings 22:28, 34–38"),
    ("Was there a condition?", "An announced judgment or blessing on a people turns when the people turn", "Jer 18:7–10; Jonah 3:10; 4:2"),
    ("Is the word sworn?", "A word marked by the LORD’s oath or “will not repent” is not turned back", "Isa 54:9, 10; Mal 3:6; Ezek 24:14"),
    ("What do the letters add?", "God spoke by the prophets; the Spirit was in them; not by the will of man", "Heb 1:1; 1 Pet 1:10–12; 2 Pet 1:21"),
  ], [0.28, 0.46, 0.26]),
("p", "The volume asks to be tested. A reader who disagrees may take any row of the table and look for the verse that overturns it. If the verse is there, the position must change. If it is not, the position stands on what the text says."),
("p", "The volume has tried to be fair to the readings it opposes. Mariottini and Diamond are right that no test settles a case before the event, and the volume said so. Fortier is right that the criteria give confidence and not certainty. Wessels is right that conduct is evidence. Where the volume has pressed past them, it has done so from the sentences of the prophets, and the sentences are before the reader. If the volume has been unfair, the verses will show it."),
]

